# Mutation cases for "a signature spelled in a comment is not a file that
# reads it".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_signature_in_a_comment_is_not_a_reader.cases`.
#
# The defect was a gate that read prose as code. `workflow_trigger_coverage`
# decides which tests read inputs no `paths:` filter can enumerate by looking
# for two signatures in a test's source, and a comment is source. Measured
# 2026-08-30 on `aee11095c5`: `gate_selectors_do_not_leak.rs` names a workflow
# file in its opening comment — to say WHICH caller exports the variables it
# holds shut — and opens nothing under that directory at all. The gate demanded
# it be registered as unfilterable, which would have moved a target whose
# inputs are `sce-build/tests/**` into the workflow that runs on every push,
# the opposite of what the registry is for. Registered gates: 29. Detected once
# prose is stripped: the same 29.
#
# The mirror image of that reading had already been made, by the very file the
# gate mis-read: `gate_selectors_do_not_leak`'s own call-site scan put
# `workflow_trigger_coverage.rs` in its population because THAT file names the
# gate script in a comment, and then failed it for lacking a helper it could
# never need. Two scanners, one mistake, so the stripping is now one shared
# answer in `common::rust_source` and both scanners ask it.
#
# ⚠ Both directions are attacked below, because they fail differently and only
# one of them is loud. Stripping too LITTLE registers a gate an ordinary
# `paths:` filter already covers, and the registry test says so on the next
# push. Stripping too MUCH deletes code along with the prose, and a gate that
# genuinely sweeps the tree then reads as one that does not — which is the
# arrangement whose absence let a violation land outside every filter on
# 2026-08-04. Three of the nine cases below are that quiet direction.
#
# ⚠ A literal is copied through verbatim, so mis-reading one can change the
# answer ONLY by turning a `//` that was data into a live comment, which then
# eats the rest of the line. A first draft of the last case here SURVIVED for
# want of that: it made every apostrophe open a character literal, the scan
# swallowed the file looking for a close — and swallowing COPIES, so the marker
# was still there and no assertion moved. The fixtures were rewritten to put a
# string holding `//` behind the literal under study, which is what makes a
# mis-read observable at all.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test workflow_trigger_coverage
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs

# ── The defect itself, restored exactly ────────────────────────────
#
# The detector reads the whole file again, prose included. This is the tree as
# it stood when the gate went red, so a repair that moved the stripping
# somewhere else without calling it is caught here.

mutation_case "the detector reads the file's prose as well as its code" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/workflow_trigger_coverage.rs",
     """    let tracked_file_enumeration = concat!("ls-", "files");
    let code = code_only(src);""",
     """    let tracked_file_enumeration = concat!("ls-", "files");
    let code = src.to_string();""")
PY

# ── The stripper stops recognising a comment ───────────────────────
#
# One case per shape a comment takes, because they are separate arms and each
# can be lost on its own. A line comment is the shape the measured defect
# arrived in; a block comment nests, and stopping at the first close leaves the
# rest of the prose standing as code.

mutation_case "a line comment is left standing" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """        if s[i] == '/' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '/' {
            while i < n && s[i] != '\\n' {
                i += 1;
            }
            continue;
        }
""",
     "")
PY

mutation_case "a block comment is never entered" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """        if s[i] == '/' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '*' {
            let mut depth = 1usize;""",
     """        if s[i] == '/' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '#' {
            let mut depth = 1usize;""")
PY

mutation_case "block comments stop nesting" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """                if s[i] == '/' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '*' {
                    depth += 1;
                    i += 2;
                } else if s[i] == '*' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '/' {""",
     """                if s[i] == '*' && i + 1 < n && s[i + 1] == '/' {""")
PY

# ── The line a diagnostic points at ────────────────────────────────
#
# The sibling scan in `gate_selectors_do_not_leak` reports the LINE a bare
# shell was built on, off the stripped text. A stripper that closed the gaps
# would send a reader to the wrong line of a file that does have the defect —
# a diagnostic wrong only when it fires.

mutation_case "a block comment's newlines are dropped with it" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """                } else {
                    if s[i] == '\\n' {
                        out.push('\\n');
                    }
                    i += 1;
                }""",
     """                } else {
                    i += 1;
                }""")
PY

# ── The quiet direction: the stripper eats code ────────────────────
#
# Each of these mis-reads one kind of literal, and the fixture behind it holds
# a `//` the mis-reading turns live.

mutation_case "an escaped quote closes the string it sits in" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """                if s[i] == '\\\\' && i + 1 < n {
                    out.push(s[i]);
                    out.push(s[i + 1]);
                    i += 2;
                    continue;
                }
                out.push(s[i]);
                i += 1;
                if s[i - 1] == '"' {""",
     """                out.push(s[i]);
                i += 1;
                if s[i - 1] == '"' {""")
PY

mutation_case "a raw string is read as an ordinary one" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """    if j >= s.len() || s[j] != 'r' {
        return None;
    }""",
     """    if j >= s.len() || s[j] != 'R' {
        return None;
    }""")
PY

mutation_case "a character literal is read as a lifetime" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """    i + 2 < s.len() && s[i + 2] == '\\''
}""",
     """    false
}""")
PY

# ── And the other half of that same decision ───────────────────────
#
# A lifetime read as a literal does not eat the file — it copies it — so what
# it loses is the STRIPPING, and the prose it swallowed comes back as a read.

mutation_case "a lifetime is read as a character literal" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/common/rust_source.rs",
     """    i + 2 < s.len() && s[i + 2] == '\\''
}""",
     """    true
}""")
PY
